```groovy
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Run Selenium Tests') {
            steps {
                sh 'mvn clean test -DsuiteXmlFile=testng.xml'
            }
        }
    }

    post {
        always {
            script {
                def comment = "Selenium tests passed! 🎉"
                def prId = env.GHPRB_PULL_ID
                def githubToken = 'YOUR_GITHUB_TOKEN'

                def url = "https://api.github.com/repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/${prId}/comments"
                def headers = [
                    'Authorization': "token ${githubToken}",
                    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
                ]
                def body = [
                    body: comment
                ]

                def response = hudson.plugins.github.GithubPlugin.getGitHub().createComment(url, headers, body)

                if (response.statusCode == 201) {
                    echo 'Comment posted successfully!'
                } else {
                    echo "Error posting comment: ${response.content}"
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we're using the `hudson.plugins.github.GithubPlugin` class to interact with the GitHub API. We're setting up the comment content, pull request ID, and GitHub access token, and then sending the comment to GitHub using the `createComment` method.

Note that you'll need to replace `YOUR_GITHUB_TOKEN`, `OWNER`, and `REPO` with your own values. Your GitHub token can be generated in your account settings.

This pipeline will automatically add a comment to the corresponding pull request indicating whether the Selenium tests passed or failed.

